    name: 'Erich Maria Remarque',
    bio: "Erich Maria Remarque (1898–1970) was a German novelist born in Osnabrück, Germany. He was conscripted into the German Army in 1916 at the age of eighteen and served on the Western Front, where he was wounded several times before the end of the war. He worked as a journalist and racing car driver before publishing All Quiet on the Western Front (Im Westen nichts Neues) in 1929, which was an immediate international sensation: it sold a million copies in its first year, was translated into more than twenty languages, and was adapted into a celebrated American film in 1930. The Nazis burned it in 1933, stripped Remarque of his German citizenship in 1938, and executed his sister, Elfriede Scholz, in 1943 partly in retaliation for his anti-Nazi writing. Remarque himself had emigrated to Switzerland in 1931 and eventually settled in the United States, where he became friends with F. Scott Fitzgerald and Marlene Dietrich, with whom he had a long relationship. His subsequent novels include The Road Back (1931), Three Comrades (1936), Arch of Triumph (1945), A Time to Love and a Time to Die (1954), and The Night in Lisbon (1961) — all concerned with the consequences of war and fascism for ordinary people. He died in Locarno, Switzerland, in 1970. All Quiet on the Western Front remains one of the most widely read novels in the world and is a standard text in secondary schools across Europe and North America.",
