    name: 'Jonathan Franzen',
    bio: "Jonathan Franzen (born 1959) is an American novelist born in Western Springs, Illinois, and raised in Webster Groves, Missouri — a suburban St. Louis community that shapes the setting and emotional world of The Corrections. He studied German literature at Swarthmore College and spent several years in Germany before returning to the United States. His first two novels, The Twenty-Seventh City (1988) and Strong Motion (1992), were well received but not widely read. The Corrections (2001), his third novel, won the National Book Award, was shortlisted for the Pulitzer Prize, became a New York Times bestseller, and sparked a widely reported public controversy when Franzen expressed ambivalence about Oprah Winfrey's selection of the novel for her book club. The novel's success made Franzen one of the most prominent American literary novelists of his generation and the subject of significant cultural discussion about the place of serious fiction in American life. Freedom (2010) repeated the commercial success of The Corrections and was placed on the cover of Time magazine. Crossroads (2021) began a planned trilogy. Franzen is known for his ambitious, long, and rigorously plotted novels; his essays on reading, nature, and culture (collected in How to Be Alone and The Discomfort Zone); and his work as a birder and conservationist. He is also known for what critics have called 'Franzen fatigue' — a cultural ambivalence about both his prominence and his public persona.",
