    name: 'Joseph Heller',
    bio: "Joseph Heller (1923–1999) was an American novelist born in Coney Island, Brooklyn, New York, the son of Russian Jewish immigrants. He served as a bombardier in the United States Army Air Corps during World War Two, flying sixty combat missions over Italy and France, and later studied English literature at New York University, Columbia University, and Oxford. He worked as an advertising copywriter and teacher while writing Catch-22, which was published in 1961 after being rejected by several publishers who found it too unconventional. The novel sold slowly at first — it was not a bestseller on publication — but grew steadily in reputation through the 1960s, becoming a central text of antiwar feeling during the Vietnam era and selling millions of copies. Heller's subsequent novels — Something Happened (1974), Good as Gold (1979), God Knows (1984), Picture This (1988), Closing Time (1994, a sequel to Catch-22) — were commercially successful but never achieved the cultural impact of his debut. He suffered a severe attack of Guillain-Barré syndrome in 1981, which left him temporarily paralyzed; he wrote about his recovery in the memoir No Laughing Matter (1986), co-written with his friend Speed Vogel. He died in East Hampton, New York, in 1999. The phrase 'Catch-22' entered the English language as a general term for any no-win paradox created by contradictory rules, and the novel is considered one of the most important American novels of the twentieth century.",
