    name: 'Kurt Vonnegut',
    bio: "Kurt Vonnegut (1922–2007) was an American novelist born in Indianapolis, Indiana, into a German-American family. He studied biochemistry at Cornell University before being drafted into the United States Army in 1943. He was captured by German forces during the Battle of the Bulge in December 1944 and held as a prisoner of war in Dresden, where he survived the Allied firebombing of February 13–15, 1945 in an underground slaughterhouse — the event that became the subject of Slaughterhouse-Five. After the war he studied anthropology at the University of Chicago and worked as a journalist and public relations writer for General Electric before devoting himself to fiction. His early novels — Player Piano (1952), The Sirens of Titan (1959), Mother Night (1962), Cat's Cradle (1963) — were published as science fiction paperback originals and did not reach a wide audience. God Bless You, Mr. Rosewater (1965) and Slaughterhouse-Five (1969) transformed his reputation: Slaughterhouse-Five became an immediate bestseller, and Vonnegut became one of the most widely read American writers of his generation, particularly on college campuses during the Vietnam War. His subsequent novels — Breakfast of Champions (1973), Slapstick (1976), Jailbird (1979), Deadeye Dick (1982) — maintained his prominence but were considered less successful than his peak work. He also published several essay collections — Wampeters, Foma & Granfalloons (1974), Palm Sunday (1981), Fates Worse Than Death (1991), A Man Without a Country (2005) — which showed his development as an essayist and social critic. He died in New York in 2007 after a fall at his home. He is remembered for his satirical wit, his humanism, and the refrain 'So it goes,' which has become one of the most recognized phrases in American literature.",
