    name: 'Don DeLillo',
    bio: "Don DeLillo (born 1936) is an American novelist born in the Bronx, New York, into an Italian immigrant family. He attended Fordham University in the Bronx, where he studied communication arts, and worked in advertising before turning to fiction. His early novels — Americana (1971), End Zone (1972), Great Jones Street (1973), Ratner's Star (1976), Players (1977), Running Dog (1978) — were well received by critics but not widely read. White Noise (1985), his eighth novel, won the National Book Award, reached a broad audience, and established him as one of the major American novelists of his generation. Libra (1988), a novel about the Kennedy assassination with Lee Harvey Oswald as protagonist, was a bestseller and critical success. Mao II (1991) won the PEN/Faulkner Award. Underworld (1997), a vast American epic moving backward from 1992 to 1951 — his most ambitious novel — was shortlisted for the Pulitzer Prize and debuted at number one on the New York Times bestseller list. Subsequent novels — The Body Artist (2001), Cosmopolis (2003), Falling Man (2007), Point Omega (2010), Zero K (2016), The Silence (2020) — have maintained his reputation, though Underworld and White Noise are generally considered his masterworks. DeLillo is known for his interest in paranoia, celebrity, media, violence, and the systems that organize contemporary American life; his prose style is distinctive for its rhythmic precision and its attention to the language of consumer culture. He received the PEN/Saul Bellow Award for Achievement in American Fiction in 2010.",
