    name: 'John Kennedy Toole',
    bio: "John Kennedy Toole (1937–1969) was an American novelist born in New Orleans, Louisiana, the only child of parents who were the primary audience of his precocious wit. He studied English at Tulane University and Columbia University, taught briefly, and served in the United States Army in Puerto Rico. He began writing A Confederacy of Dunces in the early 1960s and submitted it to the publisher Simon & Schuster, where he developed a correspondence with the editor Robert Gottlieb; after lengthy negotiations Gottlieb ultimately rejected the novel. Toole continued to revise it but found no other publisher interested. He suffered from depression, paranoia (he became convinced that Walker Percy had plagiarized him and that forces were conspiring against his career), and alcoholism. In March 1969, at the age of thirty-one, he drove to Biloxi, Mississippi, and died by suicide in his car. His mother, Thelma Toole, spent the following decade attempting to place the novel. She finally reached Walker Percy, then teaching at Loyola University of the South in New Orleans, and pressed the manuscript on him. Percy was moved by the novel and championed its publication at Louisiana State University Press in 1980. A Confederacy of Dunces won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1981. Toole's only other completed work, The Neon Bible (written in 1954, published 1989), is a far simpler novel written when he was sixteen. He left no other fiction. A Confederacy of Dunces has sold millions of copies and is one of the most beloved comic novels in American literature.",
