    name: 'Norman Mailer',
    bio: "Norman Mailer (1923–2007) was an American novelist, journalist, filmmaker, and public intellectual, and one of the most prominent American literary figures of the twentieth century. Born in New Jersey, he studied aeronautical engineering at Harvard University before serving as an infantryman in the Pacific theater during World War Two. His first novel, The Naked and the Dead (1948), based partly on his war service, was published when he was twenty-five and became an immediate bestseller — one of the most widely read American war novels of the twentieth century. His subsequent career was prolific, controversial, and formally experimental: the essays collected in Advertisements for Myself (1959), The Armies of the Night (1968, Pulitzer Prize), Miami and the Siege of Chicago (1968), and The Executioner's Song (1979, Pulitzer Prize) helped define the New Journalism alongside Tom Wolfe and Hunter S. Thompson. His fiction after The Naked and the Dead included Barbary Shore (1951), The Deer Park (1955), An American Dream (1965), Why Are We in Vietnam? (1967), and Harlot's Ghost (1991). He was a co-founder of The Village Voice and twice president of the American chapter of PEN International. His personal life was as turbulent as his literary career: he was married six times and in 1960 stabbed his second wife at a party, for which he received a suspended sentence.",
