    name: 'Albert Camus',
    bio: "Albert Camus (1913–1960) was a French Algerian novelist, playwright, and essayist, and the second-youngest recipient of the Nobel Prize in Literature (awarded in 1957, when he was forty-four). Born in Mondovi, Algeria, into a working-class family — his father was killed at the Battle of the Marne in 1914, before Camus was a year old; his mother was partially deaf and illiterate — he was raised in poverty in the Belcourt district of Algiers. He excelled at school and was taken under the wing of his teacher Louis Germain, to whom he dedicated his Nobel address. He contracted tuberculosis at seventeen, which prevented him from sitting his teacher's examination and shaped his engagement with mortality throughout his life. He studied philosophy at the University of Algiers and wrote his first major works there before emigrating to France in 1940. The Stranger (L'Étranger) and the philosophical essay The Myth of Sisyphus were both published in 1942, establishing the concept of 'the absurd' that would define his reputation. The Plague (La Peste, 1947) and The Fall (La Chute, 1956) followed. He was associated with the French Resistance during the German occupation and edited the clandestine newspaper Combat. His philosophical disputes with Jean-Paul Sartre over the question of political violence and communism, conducted publicly in the early 1950s, were the defining intellectual controversy of postwar French culture. He was killed in a car accident near Sens on January 4, 1960; he was forty-six. His Nobel lecture, delivered in 1957, remains one of the most moving statements of the writer's moral responsibility in an era of violence.",
