    name: 'Juan Rulfo',
    bio: "Juan Rulfo (1917–1986) was a Mexican novelist and short story writer whose two slender works of fiction — the short story collection El Llano en llamas (The Burning Plain, 1953) and the novel Pedro Páramo (1955) — are among the most influential texts in twentieth-century Spanish-language literature. Born in Apulco, in the state of Jalisco, he was orphaned early (his father was murdered during the Cristero War, his mother died shortly after) and raised partly in an orphanage and by relatives. He moved to Mexico City in his teens and worked as an immigration officer and later for a rubber company while writing. The Burning Plain was published when he was thirty-six and Pedro Páramo when he was thirty-seven. After Pedro Páramo, Rulfo published almost nothing — a screenplay, a few photographic works, occasional short pieces — spending the remaining thirty years of his life at the Instituto Nacional Indigenista in Mexico City. The silence has been the subject of considerable speculation. Gabriel García Márquez famously said that when he first read Pedro Páramo 'in a single afternoon,' he turned around and reread it that same evening, and that he spent the following year learning it by memory before he could begin writing One Hundred Years of Solitude. Rulfo's influence on Latin American and world literature is entirely disproportionate to the quantity of his published work.",
