    name: 'Thomas Mann',
    bio: "Thomas Mann (1875–1955) was a German novelist, short story writer, and essayist, and one of the towering figures of twentieth-century world literature. Born in Lübeck into a prosperous merchant family — the Manns of Lübeck are the direct model for the Buddenbrooks — he was the second of five children, and his elder brother Heinrich Mann was also a significant novelist. Buddenbrooks (1901), published when he was twenty-five, was an immediate success and established him as Germany's leading young novelist. His early short fiction, including Tonio Kröger (1903) and Death in Venice (Der Tod in Venedig, 1912), confirmed and extended his reputation. The Magic Mountain (Der Zauberberg, 1924), written in the shadow of World War One, is his most ambitious and philosophically rich novel: Hans Castorp's seven years in a Swiss tuberculosis sanatorium, educated by the competing ideologies of the liberal humanist Settembrini and the irrationalist Jesuit Naphta. Mann was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1929, with Buddenbrooks cited as the primary reason. He was forced into exile by the Nazis in 1933, living first in Switzerland and then in the United States, where he eventually settled in Pacific Palisades, California. His late major works include the Joseph and His Brothers tetralogy (1933–1943), Doctor Faustus (1947), and The Holy Sinner (1951). He is one of the few writers of his century who can claim a significant place in both the nineteenth-century realist tradition (Buddenbrooks) and the twentieth-century modernist tradition (Doctor Faustus).",
