    name: 'Malcolm Lowry',
    bio: "Malcolm Lowry (1909–1957) was an English novelist, poet, and short story writer, best known for Under the Volcano (1947), one of the most discussed and debated novels of the twentieth century. Born in Birkenhead, Cheshire, into a wealthy Liverpool cotton merchant family, he was educated at Cambridge but spent his formative years before university as a merchant sailor — his first novel, Ultramarine (1933), drew directly on this experience. He was an alcoholic throughout his adult life, and alcoholism both provided the subject matter of Under the Volcano and made the novel almost impossible to write: he worked on it for over a decade through at least seven complete drafts, living in Mexico, New York, France, and finally in a remote squatter's shack on Burrard Inlet in British Columbia, Canada, with his second wife Margerie Bonner. The novel was rejected by twelve publishers before Jonathan Cape accepted a revised version in 1947. After its publication, Lowry was unable to complete another major work; the manuscripts he left at his death, found in a disordered state in the shack, have been partially published as Hear Us O Lord from Heaven Thy Dwelling Place (1961) and October Ferry to Gabriola (1970). He died at his cottage in Ripe, Sussex, in 1957, at the age of forty-seven; the inquest verdict was 'death by misadventure' (an overdose of gin and sleeping tablets). Under the Volcano has since been ranked on multiple occasions as one of the hundred greatest English-language novels of the twentieth century.",
