    name: 'Evelyn Waugh',
    bio: "Evelyn Waugh (1903–1966) was an English novelist whose career encompassed some of the funniest comic writing of the twentieth century and some of its most moving Catholic fiction. Born in Hampstead, London, into a literary family — his father Arthur Waugh was a publisher and his brother Alec Waugh was a novelist — he was educated at Lancing College and Hertford College, Oxford, where he read history without distinction and spent much of his time in artistic and social circles. He worked briefly as a schoolmaster in Wales, an experience that provided material for his first novel, Decline and Fall (1928), written at twenty-three; the novel's success established him as a comic satirist of English upper-class life. Vile Bodies (1930), Black Mischief (1932), A Handful of Dust (1934), and Scoop (1938) followed in rapid succession. In 1930, Waugh converted to Catholicism, which would become the central fact of his mature fiction and his personal life. He served in the Commandos during World War Two, an experience he processed in the Sword of Honour trilogy (Men at Arms, 1952; Officers and Gentlemen, 1955; Unconditional Surrender, 1961). Brideshead Revisited (1945), written during the war and the most autobiographically personal of his novels, marked his transition from satirist to elegist: a novel about the working of divine grace through an aristocratic Catholic family and the narrator's gradual conversion. The Ordeal of Gilbert Pinfold (1957), a thinly veiled account of his own breakdown, is among the most disturbing of his works. He is now considered one of the finest English prose stylists of the century, and his comic novels remain as sharp as when they were written.",
