    name: 'José Saramago',
    bio: "José Saramago (1922–2010) was a Portuguese novelist, playwright, poet, and journalist who became the only Portuguese-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, awarded in 1998. Born in Azinhaga, a small village in the Ribatejo province of Portugal, into a peasant family, he spent his youth in Lisbon, where he worked as a car mechanic, civil servant, journalist, and translator before becoming a full-time writer in his fifties. His early novels attracted little attention; it was Baltasar and Blimunda (1982), a historical novel set in eighteenth-century Portugal, that established his distinctive narrative voice — long, unpunctuated paragraphs, dialogue embedded in prose without conventional attribution, a narrator who interrupts to comment and digress. The Gospel According to Jesus Christ (1991), which presented Jesus as fully human and uncertain of his mission, caused such controversy with the Portuguese Catholic authorities that Saramago left Portugal for the island of Lanzarote in the Canary Islands, where he spent the rest of his life. Blindness (1995), his most internationally celebrated novel, follows an epidemic of white blindness through an unnamed city, using the pandemic as a parable about civilization's fragility. The Stone Raft (1986), The History of the Siege of Lisbon (1989), All the Names (1997), The Cave (2000), Seeing (2004), and Death with Interruptions (2005) followed. He was a committed Communist throughout his life, and his politics are embedded in his fiction without ever reducing it to polemic. He died on Lanzarote at eighty-seven, completing his memoir Cain (2009) in his final year.",
