    name: 'Knut Hamsun',
    bio: "Knut Hamsun (1859–1952) was a Norwegian novelist, poet, and playwright whose literary innovations — particularly the stream-of-consciousness technique introduced in Hunger (1890) — made him one of the founding figures of literary modernism and one of the most influential writers of the twentieth century. Born Knud Pedersen in Lom, Gudbrandsdal, he spent his childhood in poverty in northern Norway and his young adulthood working a series of laboring jobs in Norway and, twice, in America. The experiences of poverty and humiliation in the 1880s, spent in Christiania (Oslo) trying to establish himself as a writer, directly fed Hunger, written in fragments published in a Danish journal and then revised as his first novel. Its radically interior first-person narration of a starving, hallucinating artist who refuses to compromise his pride was something entirely new in prose fiction. Mysteries (1892), Pan (1894), Victoria (1898), and the major late novel Growth of the Soil (1917) — for which he received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1920 — followed. His influence on Kafka, Beckett, Henry Miller, Paul Auster, and the entire tradition of alienated literary modernism has been enormous. His late life was catastrophically compromised by his support for the Nazi occupation of Norway: he met Hitler, published pro-occupation articles, and sent his Nobel medal to Josef Goebbels. After the war he was tried for collaboration and ordered to pay a large fine. He died at ninety-two, partially rehabilitated but never fully forgiven.",
