    name: 'Richard Condon',
    bio: "Richard Condon (1915–1996) was an American novelist best known for The Manchurian Candidate (1959), a Cold War satire-thriller that imagined a Korean War veteran brainwashed by Communist captors to be a sleeper assassin. Born in New York City, he worked for twenty-one years as a Hollywood publicist and film promotion executive before turning to fiction writing at forty-two. The Manchurian Candidate was his third novel but his breakthrough: its combination of political satire, thriller plot, and psychological horror — including the unforgettable Queen of Diamonds trigger device — made it a literary event and then, with John Frankenheimer's 1962 film adaptation, a cultural touchstone. The novel was withdrawn from circulation by the Sinatra estate following the assassination of President Kennedy and did not return to print until 1988. Condon's subsequent career was prolific: Winter Kills (1974), another political assassination novel; Prizzi's Honor (1982) and its sequels, a Mafia comedy series; and Mile High (1969) and The Whisper of the Axe (1976). Prizzi's Honor was adapted by John Huston into a 1985 film starring Jack Nicholson and Anjelica Huston. Condon was deeply influenced by the satirical tradition of Evelyn Waugh and Graham Greene, and his best work combines genuine thriller craft with a savage critique of American political culture. He died in Dallas at eighty.",
