    name: 'John Fowles',
    bio: "John Fowles (1926–2005) was an English novelist whose formally ambitious and intellectually challenging fiction — particularly The Collector (1963), The Magus (1965), and The French Lieutenant's Woman (1969) — made him one of the most important British novelists of the postwar period. Born in Leigh-on-Sea, Essex, he served in the Royal Marines before taking a degree in French at New College, Oxford. He worked as a schoolteacher for many years — teaching English in Greece, which provided the setting for The Magus, and later at various English schools — while writing in his spare time. The Collector, his debut, was immediately recognized as a disturbing masterpiece: the story of a lonely butterfly collector who kidnaps an art student and the girl's diary account of her captivity set a new standard for the psychological thriller. The Magus (1965), about a young Englishman on a Greek island subjected to an elaborate game of illusion by a mysterious millionaire, was his most ambitious and most debated novel; he revised it substantially for a 1977 edition. The French Lieutenant's Woman (1969), in which a narrator who announces himself as the author comments on and occasionally interferes with a Victorian love story, was his most commercially successful and critically celebrated work, winning the W.H. Smith Literary Award. He also wrote The Ebony Tower (1974), Daniel Martin (1977), Mantissa (1982), and A Maggot (1985). He lived for most of his adult life in Lyme Regis, Dorset, which inspired the setting of The French Lieutenant's Woman, and became a significant figure in the town's cultural life.",
