    bio: "Charles Portis (1933–2020) was an American novelist and journalist whose five novels — particularly True Grit (1968) and Dog of the South (1979) — earned him a small but passionate readership that considered him one of the funniest and most distinctive American prose stylists of the twentieth century. Born in El Dorado, Arkansas, he served in the Marine Corps in Korea and studied journalism at the University of Arkansas. He worked as a reporter for the New York Herald Tribune in the late 1950s and early 1960s, reporting from London and becoming a close friend of Tom Wolfe's before the New Journalism movement he helped inspire. He returned to Arkansas in the mid-1960s to write fiction. True Grit (1968), about a fourteen-year-old Arkansas girl who hires a one-eyed marshal to pursue her father's killer, was immediately recognized as a masterpiece of American vernacular fiction and was serialized in The Saturday Evening Post before book publication; the 1969 John Wayne film and the 2010 Coen Brothers adaptation both drew new readers to the novel. His four other novels — Norwood (1966), Dog of the South (1979), Masters of Atlantis (1985), and Gringos (1991) — were loved by their readers but rarely achieved the recognition True Grit brought. The New Yorker published a long appreciation in 1998 that began a slow critical rehabilitation; by the time of his death in Little Rock at eighty-six, he was widely recognized as an underrated American master.",
