    name: 'Patricia Highsmith',
    bio: "Patricia Highsmith (1921–1995) was an American novelist and short story writer whose psychological crime fiction — particularly the five Tom Ripley novels — transformed the thriller genre by centering the criminal perspective and stripping away conventional moral framing. Born Mary Patricia Plangman in Fort Worth, Texas, she was raised primarily by her grandmother after her parents divorced, and adopted the name Highsmith from her stepfather. She graduated from Barnard College in 1942 and worked briefly writing comics before publishing her first novel, Strangers on a Train (1950), which Alfred Hitchcock adapted into a film in 1951. The novel, about two men who meet on a train and agree to exchange murders, introduced her signature theme: the violence hidden beneath the surface of ordinary American life. The Talented Mr. Ripley (1955) followed, introducing Tom Ripley, a charming sociopath who kills his way into the life he wants and is never caught. Four sequels followed over the next three decades: Ripley Under Ground (1970), Ripley's Game (1974), The Boy Who Followed Ripley (1980), and Ripley Under Water (1991). Highsmith emigrated to Europe in the early 1960s, living in England, France, Germany, and Switzerland, and her expatriate perspective increasingly informed her fiction. The Price of Salt (1952), published pseudonymously as Claire Morgan, was one of the first lesbian novels to portray a same-sex relationship without tragedy or punishment; she acknowledged authorship only in 1990. She died in Locarno, Switzerland, at seventy-four.",
