    name: 'Umberto Eco',
    bio: "Umberto Eco (1932–2016) was an Italian novelist, semiotician, literary critic, and cultural theorist whose debut novel, The Name of the Rose (1980), was an international bestseller that brought medieval detective fiction to a mass audience and established him as one of the most important literary voices of the late twentieth century. Born in Alessandria, Piedmont, he studied philosophy and medieval aesthetics at the University of Turin, where he wrote his doctoral thesis on the aesthetics of Thomas Aquinas. He worked in Italian television production and taught semiotics at the University of Bologna, where he became one of the world's foremost authorities on the theory of signs and communication. His academic works — A Theory of Semiotics (1976), The Role of the Reader (1979), Semiotics and the Philosophy of Language (1984) — were highly influential in literary theory before The Name of the Rose brought him general readership. The novel's labyrinthine monastery, its Sherlock Holmes-inflected protagonist, and its examination of the medieval relationship between knowledge and power were an immediate sensation: it spent two years on bestseller lists across Europe and was translated into over forty languages. Foucault's Pendulum (1988), The Island of the Day Before (1994), Baudolino (2000), The Mysterious Flame of Queen Loana (2004), The Prague Cemetery (2010), and Numero Zero (2015) followed. He died in Milan at eighty-four, a few weeks after completing what became his last public interview.",
