    name: 'Nathanael West',
    bio: "Nathanael West (1903–1940) was an American novelist whose four short novels — particularly Miss Lonelyhearts (1933) and The Day of the Locust (1939) — are among the most technically accomplished dark comedies in American literature, though he was largely unrecognized during his lifetime. Born Nathan Weinstein in New York City to immigrant Jewish parents, he attended Brown University and spent time in Paris before working as the manager of two New York hotels in the early Depression years, where he observed the lives of the down-and-out who became his literary subjects and gave free rooms to writers including Dashiell Hammett and James Agee. Miss Lonelyhearts, his second novel, was inspired by a conversation with a friend who worked on an actual advice column; the novel of a columnist slowly destroyed by the suffering in the letters he receives was nearly ignored on publication but is now recognized as a masterpiece. After moving to Hollywood to work as a screenwriter in 1935, West wrote The Day of the Locust (1939), a savage portrait of the violence beneath Hollywood's glamour. He died in a car accident in El Centro, California, on December 22, 1940 — one day after the death of F. Scott Fitzgerald, whom he knew. He was thirty-seven. His influence on Flannery O'Connor, Thomas Pynchon, Don DeLillo, and American dark comedy has been enormous, and his rehabilitation as a major American writer was largely posthumous.",
