    name: 'Joanne Harris',
    bio: "Joanne Harris (born 1964) is a British author of French descent best known for Chocolat (1999), a magical fable about a chocolatier who arrives in a small French village during Lent and disrupts its repressive social order. Born in Barnsley, Yorkshire, to a French mother and an English father who owned a sweet shop — a detail that feels almost too neat given her literary preoccupations — she studied modern and medieval languages at Cambridge and taught French at Leeds Grammar School for fifteen years before Chocolat's success allowed her to write full time. The novel, rejected by multiple publishers before being accepted, became an international bestseller and was adapted by Lasse Hallström into a 2000 film starring Juliette Binoche and Alfred Molina, which was nominated for five Academy Awards. Harris continued the Vianne Thierry series with The Lollipop Shoes (2007) and Peaches for Monsieur le Curé (2012). Her other novels — Blackberry Wine (2000), Five Quarters of the Orange (2001), Holy Fools (2003), Coastliners (2002) — explore similar territory: food, magic, French settings, and the tension between sensuality and repression. She has also written fantasy novels under the pen name J.J. Cooper and the Gospel of Loki (2014) and its sequels. She is a passionate advocate for the reading public and regularly engages with readers online.",
