    bio: "Larry McMurtry (1936–2021) was an American novelist, essayist, and screenwriter from Archer City, Texas, whose work across more than thirty novels defined a distinctly American literary tradition at the intersection of the Western genre and serious fiction. Born and raised on a ranch in north Texas, he attended North Texas State University and Rice University, and began publishing fiction in the early 1960s with a series of novels set in Texas that were critically acclaimed from the outset. Horseman, Pass By (1961), filmed as Hud (1963), and The Last Picture Show (1966) established him as a major chronicler of the changing American West — specifically of the decline of the cattle ranching culture that had shaped his own childhood and that he regarded with profound ambivalence. His most celebrated novel, Lonesome Dove (1985), a Pulitzer Prize-winning epic following two aging Texas Rangers on a cattle drive from the Rio Grande to Montana, was the culmination of his engagement with Western myth and the novel he had been building toward for two decades. It became one of the bestselling and most beloved American novels of the century and was adapted into a landmark 1989 television miniseries. McMurtry later expanded Lonesome Dove into a four-book saga — adding the sequel Streets of Laredo (1993) and the prequels Dead Man's Walk (1995) and Comanche Moon (1997). He was also a prolific screenwriter; he won the Academy Award for Best Adapted Screenplay for Brokeback Mountain (2005). He spent the last decades of his life in Archer City, where he operated a used bookstore.",
