    name: 'James Clavell',
    bio: "James Clavell (1921–1994) was a British-American novelist, screenwriter, and film director whose Asian Saga — six novels set across Asia over four centuries — made him one of the bestselling novelists of the twentieth century. Born Charles Edmund DuMaresq Clavell in Sydney, Australia, the son of a Royal Navy officer, he served as an officer in the Royal Artillery during World War II and was captured by Japanese forces in Singapore in 1942, spending the remainder of the war as a prisoner of war in Changi prison camp under conditions of extreme deprivation. This experience — which killed the majority of his fellow prisoners — became the subject of his first novel, King Rat (1962), and remained the defining fact of his artistic imagination: a lifelong fascination with the collision of Western and Asian cultures, with the codes of honor and power that governed different societies, and with the question of how individuals survive within total systems. Tai-Pan (1966), set in the founding of British Hong Kong, established him as a commercial force, but Shōgun (1975) — the story of an English navigator shipwrecked in feudal Japan in 1600 who enters the service of a powerful warlord — was his masterpiece: a meticulous, immersive, and compulsively readable epic that became one of the bestselling novels of the twentieth century and the basis of a celebrated 1980 television miniseries and an Emmy-winning 2024 FX/Hulu series. Noble House (1981), Taikō (1982), Whirlwind (1986), and Gai-Jin (1993) completed the Asian Saga. Clavell also wrote the screenplays for The Great Escape (1963) and directed To Sir, with Love (1967).",
