    name: 'Italo Calvino',
    bio: "Italo Calvino (1923–1985) was an Italian novelist and short story writer whose work evolved from neorealism through fable and allegory to a distinctive form of postmodern fiction that combined intellectual playfulness with lyrical precision, making him one of the most influential European writers of the second half of the twentieth century. Born in Cuba and raised in San Remo, he joined the Italian Communist Party at the end of World War II and participated in the partisan resistance movement, experiences that shaped his early realistic fiction — the novella The Path to the Spiders' Nests (1947) — before he moved toward the fantastical with the folk-inflected Our Ancestors trilogy: The Viscount Who Was Cut in Half (1952), The Baron in the Trees (1957), and The Nonexistent Knight (1959). His reputation as an international literary figure was established by Cosmicomics (1965), a series of stories narrated by a being who witnesses cosmological events, and secured by Invisible Cities (1972), in which Marco Polo describes imaginary cities to Kublai Khan. The Castle of Crossed Destinies (1969), in which a group of travelers who have lost the power of speech use tarot cards to tell their stories, further developed his interest in combinatorial structures and the relationship between form and meaning. If on a winter's night a traveler (1979), a second-person novel addressed to 'the Reader,' became his most celebrated and widely translated work. He spent the years from 1967 onward partly in Paris, where he was associated with the Oulipo group of writers committed to formal constraints. He died of a brain hemorrhage in Siena in September 1985, while preparing the Norton Lectures he was to deliver at Harvard.",
