    name: 'Charlotte Brontë',
    bio: "Charlotte Brontë (1816–1855) was an English novelist and poet who published under the male pseudonym Currer Bell before her identity became known, and whose Jane Eyre (1847) established her as one of the most important novelists in the English language. Born at Thornton in the West Riding of Yorkshire, the third of six children of an Irish clergyman, she spent most of her adult life at Haworth Parsonage on the Yorkshire moors, the landscape that saturated the work of all three Brontë sisters. After several years working as a teacher and governess, Charlotte and her sisters Emily and Anne decided to publish a joint collection of poetry — Poems by Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell (1846) — which sold poorly. All three then turned to fiction: Charlotte's The Professor was rejected by publishers, but Jane Eyre, written in the same period, was accepted and published in 1847 to immediate popular and critical success. Jane Eyre narrates in the first person the life of a plain, poor, morally serious governess who falls in love with her wealthy and brooding employer, Rochester, and is forced to choose between her integrity and her desire — a story whose combination of Gothic romance, psychological intensity, and feminist assertion of female dignity made it one of the most beloved and influential novels ever written. Shirley (1849) and Villette (1853) followed. Charlotte outlived all her siblings — Emily died in 1848, Branwell and Anne in 1849 — and married her father's curate, Arthur Bell Nicholls, in 1854; she died the following year, likely of complications from pregnancy.",
