    bio: "Emily Brontë (1818–1848) was an English novelist and poet who published under the male pseudonym Ellis Bell, and whose single novel, Wuthering Heights (1847), is one of the most powerful and enduring works in the English language. Born at Thornton in Yorkshire, the fifth of six children of a clergyman, she spent almost her entire life at Haworth Parsonage on the Yorkshire moors, which provided the landscape and the weather of her imagination. Unlike her sister Charlotte, who attended school and worked as a teacher and governess, Emily was intensely domestic and rarely left home; she suffered acutely on the few occasions she did, including a brief period as a teacher at Law Hill school that she found intolerable. Her poetry, published in the joint Poems by Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell (1846), is now recognized as among the finest Victorian lyric verse. Wuthering Heights, published in 1847, tells the story of the foundling Heathcliff and his obsessive love for Catherine Earnshaw, spanning two generations and narrated through a complex frame structure that many Victorian reviewers found confusing and morally objectionable — the novel was widely criticized on publication for its apparent endorsement of Heathcliff's passion and its refusal of conventional moral resolution. Emily Brontë died of tuberculosis in December 1848, at thirty years old, having published no other fiction; she declined medical treatment to the end. Wuthering Heights has grown steadily in reputation since her death and is now recognized as one of the foundational texts of English Romanticism and the Gothic tradition.",
