    name: 'Mark Z. Danielewski',
    bio: "Mark Z. Danielewski (born 1966) is an American novelist whose debut novel House of Leaves (2000), initially self-distributed via the internet before publication by Pantheon Books, became one of the most discussed experimental novels in American literary history and established him as a significant figure in postmodern fiction. Born in New York City, the son of the avant-garde filmmaker Tad Danielewski and the brother of the musician Poe (with whom he collaborated on the multimedia project that accompanied House of Leaves), he studied at Yale University and the University of Southern California. House of Leaves was his first novel; it had circulated in fragments on the internet for several years before formal publication and already had a devoted following. The novel's complex typographical experiments — footnotes nested within footnotes, text running in multiple directions, pages deliberately laid out to disorient — and its layered fictional apparatus (a blind academic's scholarly study of a documentary film, annotated by an unreliable young man who found the manuscript) made it the subject of sustained academic attention and online communities devoted to decoding its puzzles. Only Revolutions (2006), his second novel, was shortlisted for the National Book Award. He subsequently undertook the serial novel The Familiar, an enormously ambitious twenty-seven-volume project published between 2015 and 2018 before its publisher discontinued it at volume five; the incomplete state of the project has become itself a kind of artistic object.",
