    name: 'Jean Rhys',
    bio: "Jean Rhys (1890–1979) was a British novelist born Ella Gwendoline Rees Williams in Dominica, whose work drew on her experience of poverty and displacement in early twentieth-century Europe and whose rediscovery in the 1960s made her one of the most celebrated writers of the postcolonial literary tradition. Born into a Creole family on the Caribbean island of Dominica, she moved to England at sixteen and spent the following decades moving between London and Paris in difficult circumstances, working as a chorus girl, an artist's model, and a translator, and having a series of relationships with men who alternately supported and exploited her. Her early novels — Quartet (1928), After Leaving Mr Mackenzie (1930), Voyage in the Dark (1934), and Good Morning, Midnight (1939) — drew directly on her own experience to create a series of adrift, economically dependent women of ambiguous social status in interwar Europe. After Good Morning, Midnight she largely disappeared from public view and was believed by many to be dead. Wide Sargasso Sea (1966), begun in the 1940s and completed after years of intermittent work, told the story of Antoinette Cosway — the first Mrs. Rochester, the 'madwoman in the attic' of Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre — from her own perspective. Its publication by André Deutsch won the W.H. Smith Literary Award and the Royal Society of Literature Award, and prompted the reprinting of all Rhys's earlier work. She was made a Fellow of the Royal Society of Literature and appointed CBE in 1978, a year before her death.",
