    name: 'A.S. Byatt',
    bio: "A.S. Byatt (1936–2023), full name Dame Antonia Susan Duffy (née Drabble), was a British novelist, poet, and critic whose work combined extraordinary intellectual ambition with narrative accessibility, and whose Possession (1990) won the Man Booker Prize and became one of the best-loved serious novels of its generation. Born in Sheffield, the elder sister of the novelist Margaret Drabble, she studied at Cambridge and was a university lecturer and teacher for many years before devoting herself full time to writing. Her first novel, The Shadow of a Sun (1964), was followed by The Game (1967), before the Frederica Potter quartet — The Virgin in the Garden (1978), Still Life (1985), Babel Tower (1996), and A Whistling Woman (2002) — established her as one of the major contemporary British novelists: a long, demanding, meticulously researched sequence set in the 1950s, 1960s, and 1970s, with a heroine who shares some of Byatt's own biography. Possession, a novel about two contemporary scholars uncovering a secret Victorian love affair, combined Byatt's scholarly interests (Victorian literature and culture, the history of science, fairy tale and myth) with a romance and a thriller plot that made it both critically acclaimed and widely read; it was adapted as a film in 2002. She was appointed Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 1999. Her later novels included Babel Tower and On Histories and Stories (2000), as well as numerous short story collections drawing on fairy tale traditions.",
