    name: 'Wilkie Collins',
    bio: "Wilkie Collins (1824–1889) was an English novelist and close friend of Charles Dickens whose work in the 1860s essentially invented the sensation novel and established many of the conventions of the mystery and detective genres that would dominate popular fiction for the next century and a half. Born in London, the son of the landscape painter William Collins, he studied law and was called to the bar in 1851 but never practiced, choosing instead to write. His friendship with Dickens, begun in 1851, was one of the most important literary relationships of the Victorian era; they collaborated on plays and stories, traveled together, and Dickens published Collins's work in his magazines Household Words and All the Year Round. The Woman in White, serialized in All the Year Round from November 1859 to August 1860, was a cultural sensation; readers wrote to the magazine in unprecedented numbers, and the novel generated a commercial and critical response that established Collins as one of the most important popular writers of the era. The Moonstone (1868), widely considered the first English detective novel, followed and was equally celebrated. Collins's later work — No Name (1862), Armadale (1866), The Law and the Lady (1875) — continued in the sensation vein but was less consistently successful. His personal life was unconventional for a Victorian: he maintained two separate households with two women (Martha Rudd and Caroline Graves) simultaneously for much of his adult life, and had children with both. He suffered from gout and became dependent on laudanum, which affected the quality of his later work.",
