    name: 'Mary Elizabeth Braddon',
    bio: "Mary Elizabeth Braddon (1835–1915) was an English novelist who was one of the most commercially successful writers of the Victorian era and the author of Lady Audley's Secret (1862), which became one of the bestselling novels of the nineteenth century and the defining text of the Victorian sensation novel. Born in London, the illegitimate daughter of a solicitor who abandoned his family when Braddon was five, she worked as an actress for several years in her twenties to support herself and her mother before turning to writing. Her early career was shaped by her relationship with the publisher John Maxwell, whose mentally ill wife was institutionalized; Braddon lived with Maxwell as his common-law wife (and later married him after his wife's death) and helped support his six children from his first marriage alongside their own six children together. Lady Audley's Secret — a sensation novel in which the beautiful, charming Lady Audley commits bigamy, attempted murder, and arson to protect her secret — was serialized from 1861 to 1862 and ran through eight editions in three months. It made Braddon famous and controversial in equal measure: reviewers who praised Collins's villains refused to extend the same generosity to Braddon's female one, detecting in the novel an unseemly sympathy for a woman who used feminine wiles against patriarchal authority. Braddon published more than eighty novels over her fifty-year career, including Aurora Floyd (1863), The Doctor's Wife (1864, her version of Madame Bovary), and Isadora (1884). Feminist literary criticism from the 1970s onward has substantially rehabilitated her reputation.",
