    name: 'Elizabeth Gaskell',
    bio: "Elizabeth Gaskell (1810–1865), born Elizabeth Cleghorn Stevenson, was an English novelist and short story writer whose work combined compassionate social observation with narrative skill to produce some of the most important Victorian fiction on the working-class experience of industrialization. Born in Chelsea and raised partly in Knutsford, Cheshire — the original of the fictional Hollingford in Wives and Daughters — she married the Unitarian minister William Gaskell in 1832 and settled in Manchester, where direct exposure to the conditions of the industrial poor provided the material for her first novel, Mary Barton (1848). The novel attracted enormous attention for its sympathetic treatment of factory workers' grievances and brought her to the notice of Charles Dickens, who serialized her subsequent fiction in his magazines Household Words and All the Year Round. North and South (1854–1855), which Dickens serialized under pressure of weekly deadlines she found extremely difficult, followed the clash between a clergyman's daughter from the rural south of England and the self-made mill owner John Thornton in the industrial north, using the romance between them as a vehicle for Gaskell's engagement with industrial-era class conflict. Cranford (1851–1853), a series of affectionate sketches of small-town Cheshire life, was her most popular work in her lifetime. Her biography of Charlotte Brontë (1857), commissioned by Patrick Brontë after his daughter's death, is still read as an essential account of the Brontë circle. Wives and Daughters, her final novel, left unfinished at her death, is now widely considered her masterpiece.",
