    name: 'Thomas Hardy',
    bio: "Thomas Hardy (1840–1928) was an English novelist and poet whose career spanned the late Victorian and early Modernist periods and whose fiction — set almost entirely in the semi-fictional Wessex of southwest England — produced a series of tragic masterpieces that rank among the most powerful indictments of Victorian social arrangements in the English language. Born in Higher Bockhampton, Dorset, the son of a stonemason and building contractor, he was apprenticed to a Dorchester architect at sixteen and worked in architecture for most of his twenties, moving to London in 1862 but returning to Dorset in 1867 in ill health. His early novels — Far from the Madding Crowd (1874), The Return of the Native (1878), The Mayor of Casterbridge (1886) — established him as a major regional novelist with a tragic vision and an exceptionally vivid sense of landscape. Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891), his masterpiece, follows a poor Dorset girl seduced by one man and abandoned by another, and argues through its subtitle ('A Pure Woman Faithfully Presented') that Victorian society's condemnation of fallen women was a systematic hypocrisy that punished women for what was done to them. The novel was widely praised and attacked with equal force. Jude the Obscure (1895), which followed a working-class man's doomed attempt to educate himself and love freely, was so savagely attacked by reviewers — including a bishop who publicly burned a copy — that Hardy wrote no more novels, turning entirely to poetry for the remaining thirty years of his life. He is now considered one of the two or three greatest English novelists of the nineteenth century.",
