    bio: "D.H. Lawrence (1885–1930), full name David Herbert Lawrence, was an English novelist, poet, short-story writer, and essayist whose work explored the dehumanizing effects of industrial society on human nature and relationships, and whose frank treatment of sexuality made him one of the most censored and controversially examined writers of the twentieth century. Born in Eastwood, Nottinghamshire, the fourth child of a coal miner and a former schoolteacher, he grew up in the social and economic world that his early fiction portrays: the clash between the working-class vitality of his father's world and the intellectual and social aspiration of his mother's. He won a scholarship to Nottingham High School and later studied at University College Nottingham, becoming a primary school teacher. His mother's death from cancer in 1910 was a catastrophic loss, and the consuming nature of their relationship is the subject of his autobiographical novel Sons and Lovers (1913), which established his reputation. In 1912 he eloped with Frieda von Richthofen Weekley, a German baroness and the wife of one of his professors; they married in 1914 and remained together until his death. The Rainbow (1915), about three generations of the Brangwen family, was immediately prosecuted for obscenity and destroyed; its sequel Women in Love (1920) is now widely considered his masterpiece. His later life was largely spent in voluntary exile — Italy, Australia, New Mexico, Mexico — as he sought communities and landscapes less damaged than industrial England. Lady Chatterley's Lover (1928), his final novel, was published privately in Florence but banned in Britain until 1960, when its publication by Penguin Books and the resulting obscenity trial became a defining cultural event. He died of tuberculosis in Vence, France, at the age of forty-four.",
