    name: 'Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie',
    bio: "Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie (born 1977) is a Nigerian novelist, essayist, and speaker whose work — including the novels Purple Hibiscus (2003), Half of a Yellow Sun (2006), and Americanah (2013) — has established her as one of the most prominent literary voices of her generation and a defining presence in the global conversation about race, gender, and African identity. Born in Enugu, Nigeria, and raised in Nsukka, where her father was a professor of statistics and her mother the first female registrar at the University of Nigeria, she grew up in a house that had previously belonged to the writer Chinua Achebe — an early reminder, she has said, that Africans could be central to literature rather than peripheral to it. She began studying medicine in Nigeria before moving to the United States at nineteen to study communications and political science at Drexel University, later receiving an MA in creative writing from Johns Hopkins and an MFA from Yale. Purple Hibiscus, her debut novel, won the Commonwealth Writers' Prize for Best First Book (Africa) and was shortlisted for the Orange Prize. Half of a Yellow Sun, about the Biafran War, won the Orange Prize for Fiction in 2007 and was widely recognized as a major work of historical fiction. Americanah, about a young Nigerian woman navigating race in America and love across continents, won the National Book Critics Circle Award and the Chicago Tribune Heartland Prize. Her TED talk 'We Should All Be Feminists' (2012) went viral and was adapted as a short book in 2014; Beyoncé sampled it on the track '***Flawless.' Her essay collection Notes on Grief (2021), written after her father's death during the COVID-19 pandemic, was widely praised for its directness and emotional precision. She divides her time between the United States and Nigeria.",
