    bio: "Tsitsi Dangarembga (born 1959) is a Zimbabwean novelist and filmmaker whose debut novel Nervous Conditions (1988) was the first novel published in English by a Black Zimbabwean woman and has become one of the defining works of African feminist fiction. Born in Mutoko, Southern Rhodesia (now Zimbabwe), she spent part of her childhood in England, where her father was studying, before the family returned to Rhodesia. She studied medicine briefly at the University of Zimbabwe before switching to psychology, and studied filmmaking in Germany at the Deutsche Film und Fernsehakademie. She wrote and directed the film Everyone's Child (1996), which won the FESPACO prize for Best Film from Sub-Saharan Africa. Nervous Conditions, rejected by several British publishers before being published by The Women's Press in London, was received as a major literary event and won the African section of the Commonwealth Writers' Prize. The novel took its title from Sartre's preface to Frantz Fanon's The Wretched of the Earth — 'The condition of native is a nervous condition' — and its central argument about the intersection of colonialism and patriarchy as a cause of psychological damage became one of the foundational texts of postcolonial feminist criticism. The sequel, The Book of Not (2006), appeared eighteen years later; the trilogy's final volume, This Mournable Body (2018), was shortlisted for the Booker Prize. In 2021 she was arrested in Harare while participating in an anti-corruption demonstration; her arrest drew international attention and condemnation. She lives in Harare.",
