    name: 'Michael Ondaatje',
    bio: "Michael Ondaatje (born 1943) is a Sri Lankan-Canadian novelist, poet, editor, and filmmaker whose work — including the novels Coming Through Slaughter (1976), In the Skin of a Lion (1987), The English Patient (1992), Anil's Ghost (2000), Divisadero (2007), and The Cat's Table (2011) — has established him as one of the most important literary figures in the English language of his generation. Born in Colombo, Ceylon (now Sri Lanka), the son of a tea and rubber planter and a dancer, he emigrated to England at the age of eleven following his parents' divorce, and then to Canada at nineteen, where he studied at Bishop's University and the University of Toronto and received a master's degree from Queen's University in Kingston, Ontario. He taught English at York University in Toronto for many years. His poetry, beginning with The Dainty Monsters (1967) and continuing through The Cinnamon Peeler (1991), established his reputation as a lyrical, imagistic writer whose prose style would extend the techniques of poetry into fiction. The English Patient (1992), set in an Italian villa in the final months of World War II, won the Booker Prize and was made into a successful film by Anthony Minghella in 1996; in 2018 it was named the joint winner of the Golden Man Booker Prize, an award recognizing the best Booker Prize winner of the past fifty years. Anil's Ghost (2000), about a forensic anthropologist in civil war Sri Lanka, won the Kiriyama Pacific Rim Book Prize, the Prix Médicis Étranger, and the Giller Prize. Running in the Family (1982), a fictionalized memoir of his Sri Lankan childhood, is among the most celebrated examples of literary non-fiction in Canadian letters.",
