    name: 'Margaret Drabble',
    bio: "Margaret Drabble (born 1939) is an English novelist, biographer, and literary critic whose nineteen novels — beginning with A Summer Bird-Cage (1963) and including The Millstone (1965), Jerusalem the Golden (1967), The Needle's Eye (1972), The Ice Age (1977), The Radiant Way (1987), and The Dark Flood Rises (2016) — have established her as one of the most consistent and significant English novelists of the post-war period, and whose earlier fiction in particular created an enduring portrait of the experience of educated English women in the 1960s and 1970s. Born in Sheffield, Yorkshire, the second of four children of a barrister and a teacher, she was educated at the Mount School, York (a Quaker girls' school), and Newnham College, Cambridge, where she read English and was a scholar. She briefly acted with the Royal Shakespeare Company before dedicating herself to writing; her first novel was published when she was twenty-three. The Millstone, published when she was twenty-six, drew on her experience of early motherhood and became one of the key texts of postwar British women's fiction. She was made a Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 2008. She is the sister of the novelist A.S. Byatt, and the two sisters' contrasting literary careers have been a recurring subject of literary comment; their relationship is generally described as distant. She edited the fifth and sixth editions of The Oxford Companion to English Literature (1985, 2000), a substantial scholarly achievement. She was married to the biographer Michael Holroyd from 1982 until his death in 2024.",
