    name: 'Pat Barker',
    bio: "Pat Barker (born 1943) is an English novelist whose Regeneration Trilogy — Regeneration (1991), The Eye in the Door (1993), and The Ghost Road (1995) — is widely considered one of the great achievements of late twentieth-century British fiction and the definitive literary treatment of the psychological costs of World War I. Born in Thornaby-on-Tees, County Durham, the illegitimate daughter of a working-class woman who married an older man when Pat was seven, she was educated at the London School of Economics and began writing fiction in her mid-twenties. Her early novels — Union Street (1982), Blow Your House Down (1984), and Liza's England (1986) — were working-class fiction focused on the lives of women in the north of England; they were admired but not widely read. The turning point came with Regeneration, which grew from her interest in the poet Siegfried Sassoon's declaration against the war and his treatment by the army psychologist W.H.R. Rivers at Craiglockhart War Hospital. The Ghost Road, the trilogy's conclusion, won the Booker Prize in 1995. Subsequent novels — Another World (1998), Border Crossing (2001), Double Vision (2003), Life Class (2007), Toby's Room (2012), Noonday (2015) — continued her preoccupation with trauma, violence, and the psychological aftermath of war. The Silence of the Girls (2018) and The Women of Troy (2021) retell the Iliad from the perspective of the captive Trojan women, extending her long engagement with the experience of those damaged and silenced by conflict. She was appointed Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 2000.",
