    bio: "Barbara Pym (1913–1980) was an English novelist whose twelve novels — including Excellent Women (1952), Jane and Prudence (1953), A Glass of Blessings (1958), and Quartet in Autumn (1977) — are characterized by a dry, precise comic style that has led many critics to compare her to Jane Austen, and whose literary career was interrupted for thirteen years when she found herself unable to publish before a celebrated public rediscovery in 1977. Born in Oswestry, Shropshire, the elder daughter of a solicitor, she read English at St Hilda's College, Oxford, where she became involved with a number of unsuitable men whose romantic disappointments would later furnish much of the material for her fiction. She worked for the International African Institute in London for many years as an editor of the journal Africa, and her office experience contributed to the precise social observation of her fiction. Her first six novels, published by Jonathan Cape between 1950 and 1961, received warm reviews and a devoted readership but were never bestsellers. In 1963 Cape rejected An Unsuitable Attachment, telling her that her work was no longer fashionable; she spent thirteen years unable to find a publisher. The rediscovery came in January 1977, when Philip Larkin and David Cecil independently named her as the most underrated novelist of the century in a Times Literary Supplement poll. The publication of Quartet in Autumn later that year — shortlisted for the Booker Prize — was followed by The Sweet Dove Died (1978) and A Few Green Leaves (1980). She died of cancer in Oxfordshire in January 1980, two and a half years after her restoration to the literary world. Philip Larkin called her 'the most underrated writer of the century.'",
