    bio: "Sebastian Faulks (born 1953) is an English novelist and journalist whose novel Birdsong (1993) became one of the most successful literary novels in British publishing history — selling over two million copies in the UK alone and establishing its author as one of the most widely read serious novelists in the English language. Born in Donnington, Berkshire, the son of a judge, he was educated at Wellington College and Emmanuel College, Cambridge, where he read English. He worked as a journalist for many years, becoming a features editor at The Independent and The Sunday Telegraph. His first three novels were published to little attention; Charlotte Gray was a more successful novel set in occupied France in World War II. Birdsong, his fourth novel, was published in 1993 to an enthusiastic critical reception and word-of-mouth sales that turned it into a sustained bestseller. Its combination of a pre-war love story in Amiens, the harrowing experience of the Somme, and a contemporary parallel narrative gave it a structural range that appealed to readers who do not usually read literary fiction. Charlotte Gray (1998), set in occupied France in World War II and featuring some of the French characters from Birdsong, was also a bestseller and was adapted for film in 2001. Human Traces (2005), about the early history of psychiatry, was more ambitious in scope and more mixed in reception. Engelby (2007) was a psychological thriller; A Week in December (2009) a social satire of the financial crisis. Devil May Care (2008), written as a continuation of the James Bond series authorized by the Ian Fleming Estate, was a significant commercial success. He was appointed Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 2002.",
