    bio: "Andrea Levy (1956–2019) was an English novelist whose four novels — the most celebrated of which, Small Island (2004), won the Orange Prize for Fiction, the Whitbread Novel of the Year Award, and the Commonwealth Writers' Prize — explored the experience of Caribbean immigrants in Britain and their descendants across the twentieth century. Born in London to Jamaican parents — her father was among the passengers on the HMT Empire Windrush when it docked at Tilbury in 1948 — she grew up in North London largely disconnected from her Jamaican heritage, and it was only in her thirties, after taking a creative writing course, that she began to write about the experience of being the child of Windrush-generation parents in a country that did not always recognize her as belonging to it. Her debut novel Every Light in the House Burnin' (1994) was largely autobiographical; Fruit of the Lemon (1999) explored the same territory through a protagonist who travels to Jamaica to understand where she comes from. Small Island, her third novel, reached an audience of millions in Britain and internationally and was adapted for the stage by the National Theatre in 2019, in a production that transferred to the Olivier stage the year Levy died of cancer. The Long Song (2010), set in Jamaica during slavery and its immediate aftermath, was shortlisted for the Booker Prize and showed her range beyond the British-immigrant setting of her earlier work. She was appointed Member of the Order of the British Empire in 2011.",
