    name: 'W.G. Sebald',
    bio: "W.G. Sebald — Winfried Georg Maximilian Sebald (1944–2001) — was a German novelist and academic who spent most of his working life in England, where he taught German literature at the University of East Anglia, and whose four major prose works — Vertigo (1990), The Emigrants (1992), The Rings of Saturn (1995), and Austerlitz (2001) — created a new kind of literary form combining fiction, memoir, travelogue, and historical essay in a style uniquely his own. Born in 1944 in Wertach im Allgäu, a small town in the Bavarian Alps, in the final year of the Second World War, he grew up in the postwar Germany of the economic miracle and later recalled growing up with no knowledge of the Holocaust and of what had been done in his parents' lifetime. He left Germany in 1966 to take up a teaching post in Manchester, and subsequently moved to Norwich, where he spent the rest of his career at the University of East Anglia, eventually becoming a professor of European literature and the first director of the British Centre for Literary Translation. His four major prose works were initially published to small audiences in German but found large international readerships in English translation in the late 1990s; Susan Sontag called him 'the contemporary master of the literature of lament.' The distinctive features of his prose — its long, meandering sentences; its incorporation of (apparently) authentic photographs; its meditative, peripatetic narrators; its preoccupation with memory, loss, and the long aftermath of European catastrophe — have influenced a generation of writers including Teju Cole, Rachel Cusk, and Aleksandar Hemon. He died in a car accident in Norfolk in December 2001, at the height of his international reputation.",
