    name: 'Muriel Spark',
    bio: "Muriel Spark (1918–2006) was a Scottish novelist whose twenty-two novels — most famously The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie (1961), The Girls of Slender Means (1963), The Driver's Seat (1970), and Memento Mori (1959) — are characterized by a distinctive narrative voice: precise, cold, witty, and theologically preoccupied. Born Muriel Sarah Camberg in Edinburgh, the daughter of a Jewish father and a Presbyterian mother, she was educated at James Gillespie's High School for Girls in Edinburgh — the school that provided the setting for The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie. After a failed marriage to Sydney Oswald Spark in Rhodesia, from which she returned to Britain during the Second World War, she worked in British Intelligence and then as an editor and critic. She converted to Roman Catholicism in 1954 — an event she later described as the most important of her life, and which shaped the theological underpinning of all her subsequent work. The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie (1961), which began as a short story in The New Yorker, made her reputation both critically and commercially; its serialization in the magazine and subsequent publication made it one of the best-selling literary novels of the decade. She left Britain for New York and subsequently for Italy, where she lived for most of the rest of her life, in Rome and then in Tuscany. She was appointed Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 1993. Philip Larkin admired her; Evelyn Waugh was among the writers who recognized her technical mastery. She is widely considered one of the greatest British novelists of the twentieth century.",
