    name: 'Carlos Ruiz Zafón',
    bio: "Carlos Ruiz Zafón (1964–2020) was a Spanish novelist who is best known for The Shadow of the Wind (2001), which became one of the best-selling Spanish-language novels of all time, was translated into more than 40 languages, and sold more than 15 million copies worldwide — one of the most successful debut novels in the history of Spanish literature. Born in Barcelona in 1964, Ruiz Zafón grew up in the city that would become the setting for all of his major fiction, and studied in Barcelona before moving to Los Angeles in the early 1990s, where he worked for many years as a screenwriter for film and television, writing primarily children's and young adult fiction (including The Prince of Mist, 1993) before completing The Shadow of the Wind. The novel — set in postwar Barcelona in 1945, when a ten-year-old boy named Daniel Sempere is taken by his bookseller father to the Cemetery of Forgotten Books and chooses a novel by the forgotten author Julián Carax — was first published in Spain in 2001, became a runaway bestseller, and was introduced to English-language readers by Lucia Graves's translation in 2004. The success of The Shadow of the Wind led Ruiz Zafón to expand it into a four-novel cycle set in the same Barcelona and built around the same Cemetery of Forgotten Books: The Angel's Game (2008), The Prisoner of Heaven (2011), and The Labyrinth of the Spirits (2016), the last of which he considered the best of the four. Ruiz Zafón returned to Barcelona in the final years of his life and died of colon cancer in Los Angeles in June 2020 at the age of 55.",
