    name: 'Sara Gruen',
    bio: "Sara Gruen (born 1969) is a Canadian-American novelist best known for Water for Elephants (2006), a Depression-era circus novel that spent over a year on the New York Times bestseller list, sold more than ten million copies worldwide, and was adapted into a 2011 film starring Reese Witherspoon and Robert Pattinson. Born in Vancouver, British Columbia, and raised in Ottawa, Gruen studied at Carleton University and the University of Ottawa. She worked in technical communication before turning to fiction writing; her first two novels, Riding Lessons (2004) and Flying Changes (2005), were about competitive show jumping and drew on her own experience with horses. Water for Elephants grew from Gruen's extensive research into the history of American traveling circuses in the Depression era, and the novel's authenticity — the hierarchies of circus life, the treatment of animals, the economic desperation of the period — reflects months of historical research including contemporary circus memoirs, photographs, and accounts. The novel's narrative structure — alternating between a contemporary elderly man in a nursing home and his remembered Depression-era past — allows Gruen to explore themes of memory, dignity, and the disconnect between the vividness of one's inner life and the diminishment of age. Her subsequent novels include Ape House (2010), about great apes in a language research facility, and At the Water's Edge (2015), set in wartime Scotland, both of which reflect her interest in animals and historical settings.",
