    name: 'Kathryn Stockett',
    bio: "Kathryn Stockett (born 1969) is an American novelist whose debut, The Help (2009), became one of the best-selling novels of the 2000s — spending more than 100 weeks on the New York Times bestseller list, selling more than 10 million copies in the United States, being translated into more than 35 languages, and adapted into a 2011 film that won the Academy Award for Best Supporting Actress. Born in Jackson, Mississippi, Stockett grew up in a family that employed a Black domestic worker, Demetrie McLorn, on whom she has said the character of Aibileen Clark is partly modeled. She studied English and creative writing at the University of Alabama, then worked for several years in magazine publishing in New York City. The Help — set in Jackson, Mississippi, in 1962 and told through the alternating first-person voices of Skeeter Phelan, a young white woman who wants to be a writer; Aibileen Clark, a Black maid who has raised seventeen white children; and Minny Jackson, an outspoken Black maid — was rejected by 60 literary agents before being accepted by a small publisher, Amy Einhorn Books, an imprint of G.P. Putnam's Sons, in 2009. The novel generated significant critical discussion about the ethics of a white Southern author writing in the voices of Black women, and Stockett was sued by Demetrie McLorn's niece, Ablene Cooper, who alleged that the character of Aibileen was based on her without her consent (the case was dismissed on a statute-of-limitations ruling). Stockett has said in interviews that she spent five years writing The Help and that the process of putting herself in Aibileen's voice was the central creative challenge and the most important act of empathy she has undertaken.",
