    name: 'Dan Brown',
    bio: "Dan Brown (born 1964) is an American thriller writer whose The Da Vinci Code (2003) is one of the best-selling novels of the 21st century, with more than 80 million copies sold worldwide, and whose Robert Langdon series — Angels & Demons (2000), The Da Vinci Code (2003), The Lost Symbol (2009), Inferno (2013), and Origin (2017) — has collectively sold more than 200 million copies in 56 languages. Born in Exeter, New Hampshire, the son of a mathematics professor and a church musician, Brown graduated from Phillips Exeter Academy and Amherst College, studied Spanish at the University of Seville, and spent several years in Los Angeles as a singer-songwriter before returning to New Hampshire to teach English and begin writing thrillers. His first three novels — Digital Fortress (1998), Angels & Demons (2000), and Deception Point (2001) — sold modestly, but The Da Vinci Code, published in March 2003, became a global publishing phenomenon: it spent 736 weeks on the New York Times bestseller list (the longest in the list's history), was translated into more than 50 languages, sparked international controversy over its claims about early Christianity and the Catholic Church, and was adapted into a 2006 film directed by Ron Howard and starring Tom Hanks. The novel's narrative formula — a Harvard symbologist is drawn into a global conspiracy involving secret societies, religious institutions, and historical artifacts — proved immediately replicable: each subsequent Langdon novel follows the same structure of a 24-hour race against time through famous landmarks and art, combining thriller pacing with popular-level historical and religious trivia. Brown has been criticized extensively by historians, art historians, and biblical scholars for the inaccuracy of the novel's historical and religious claims, particularly the assertion that Jesus was married to Mary Magdalene; he responds that The Da Vinci Code is a novel, not a historical document.",
