    name: 'Stieg Larsson',
    bio: "Stieg Larsson (1954–2004) was a Swedish journalist, author, and anti-fascist activist whose Millennium Trilogy — The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo (Män som hatar kvinnor, 2005), The Girl Who Played with Fire (Flickan som lekte med elden, 2006), and The Girl Who Kicked the Hornets' Nest (Luftslottet som sprängdes, 2007) — became one of the best-selling crime series in the world, catalyzing the global popularity of Nordic noir, and was published entirely posthumously, as Larsson died of a heart attack in November 2004 at the age of 50 before any of the novels appeared. Born in Skellefteå, a city in northern Sweden, Larsson was raised partly by his maternal grandparents after his parents moved away when he was an infant; the character of Lisbeth Salander, he said in interviews that were recorded before his death, was partly inspired by his reflection on what would have happened if the childhood friend who was raped in front of him — a rape he was unable to prevent — had fought back. Larsson spent his career as a journalist and editor of the anti-fascist magazine Expo, which he co-founded in 1995, and as a researcher and documentarian of far-right extremism in Sweden; his Expo work brought him death threats and the sense of personal danger that pervades the Millennium novels. The original Swedish title — Män som hatar kvinnor ('Men Who Hate Women') — makes explicit the feminist political argument that the English-language publishers softened for their markets. Larsson reportedly planned a ten-book Millennium series and completed parts of a fourth novel, which his partner Eva Gabrielsson has held but which Larsson's family — in a legally complicated situation — has declined to publish. The series was continued by Swedish author David Lagercrantz starting in 2015.",
