    name: 'Margaret Mitchell',
    bio: "Margaret Mitchell (1900–1949) was an American author whose sole novel, Gone with the Wind (1936), won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1937, has sold more than 30 million copies worldwide, was adapted into the 1939 film that won ten Academy Awards and remains one of the highest-grossing films of all time (adjusted for inflation), and is consistently ranked among the best-selling novels in American history. Born in Atlanta, Georgia, on November 8, 1900, Mitchell grew up hearing firsthand accounts of the Civil War and Reconstruction from elderly relatives and neighbors, and the novel's vision of the Old South reflects the Lost Cause mythology — the romanticized view of the antebellum plantation South as a civilization of grace and beauty destroyed by Northern aggression — that was dominant in Southern white historical consciousness in the early 20th century. Mitchell attended Smith College briefly, then returned to Atlanta following her mother's death in the 1918 flu pandemic. She married twice: first briefly to Berrien 'Red' Upshaw (1922–24), then to John Marsh, who remained her husband until her death. She worked as a feature journalist at the Atlanta Journal from 1922 to 1926 before an ankle injury forced her to stop working; during the following years, while recovering, she began writing the novel that became Gone with the Wind. The novel was written in fragments over roughly ten years; Mitchell assembled it into order and revised it in 1935 and submitted it to Macmillan editor Harold Latham, who published it in May 1936. Mitchell published nothing further; she died on August 16, 1949, when she was struck by a taxi while crossing a street in Atlanta. The Lost Cause mythology that pervades the novel — including its sympathetic portrayal of the Ku Klux Klan and its depiction of enslaved people through the 'happy slave' stereotype — has generated increasing critical controversy since the novel's publication.",
