    name: 'Alexandre Dumas',
    bio: "Alexandre Dumas (1802–1870) was a French novelist, playwright, and journalist whose The Three Musketeers (1844) and The Count of Monte Cristo (1844–46) are two of the best-selling novels in the history of French literature and among the most widely read adventure novels in any language, translated into more than 100 languages and selling hundreds of millions of copies combined. Born on July 24, 1802, in Villers-Cotterêts, a small town north of Paris, Dumas was the son of Thomas-Alexandre Dumas, a general in Napoleon's army who was one of the highest-ranking Black officers in European military history and the son of a French nobleman and an enslaved Haitian woman named Marie-Cesette Dumas. The family fell into poverty after the elder Dumas's death in 1806, and Alexandre moved to Paris in 1823 to seek his fortune. He found work as a clerk in the office of the Duc d'Orléans, read voraciously in the Bibliothèque nationale, and began writing plays; his Henri III et sa cour (1829) was a smash success at the Comédie-Française and made him famous overnight. Through the 1830s and early 1840s he produced an enormous body of dramatic work before turning primarily to the serialized historical novels for which he is best remembered. The Three Musketeers and The Count of Monte Cristo were both serialized in 1844 in Paris newspapers, written at the extraordinary pace of serial publication with the assistance of collaborators — most notably Auguste Maquet, who conceived the initial outlines of both novels. Dumas was prolific on a scale almost without precedent: he produced more than 100,000 pages of text in his lifetime, employing a 'fiction factory' of research assistants and ghost writers, which led to accusations (which he largely deflected) that many of the works published under his name were not his. Dumas was also famous for his spectacular personal prodigality — he earned vast sums and spent more, building the Château de Monte-Cristo outside Paris and dying nearly bankrupt.",
