    bio: "Bram Stoker (1847–1912) was an Irish author, theater manager, and critic whose Dracula (1897) is the foundational vampire novel in the English language and one of the most influential horror novels ever written, creating the modern mythology of the vampire and giving the genre its most enduring figure. Born Abraham Stoker on November 8, 1847, in Clontarf, a suburb of Dublin, to a Church of Ireland family, Stoker was a sickly child who was bedridden for the first seven years of his life (the cause has never been determined) before recovering completely and going on to excel in athletics at Trinity College Dublin, where he graduated in 1870 with a degree in mathematics and served as president of the Philosophical Society. He worked as a civil servant and theater critic in Dublin for a decade before becoming the personal manager and close friend of the actor Henry Irving, one of the most famous actors of the Victorian era; from 1878 until Irving's death in 1905 Stoker managed the Lyceum Theatre in London, Irving's home theatre, doing the administrative work of running one of London's premier theatrical venues while writing fiction in his spare time. Dracula, his sixth novel, was published in May 1897 to favorable reviews; it has never been out of print. The novel's epistolary format (told through journals, letters, and newspaper clippings) and its cast of characters (the solicitor Jonathan Harker, his fiancée Mina Murray, the mysterious Count who invites Harker to his Transylvanian castle, and the vampire-hunter Professor Van Helsing) became the template for vampire fiction. Stoker's other novels include The Jewel of Seven Stars (1903), a novel about Egyptian archaeology and resurrection, and The Lair of the White Worm (1911), a strange late horror novel. He died on April 20, 1912, of what may have been tertiary syphilis.",
