    bio: "Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley (1797–1851) was a British novelist, short story writer, and editor whose Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus (1818), written when she was eighteen years old, is one of the first science fiction novels, one of the foundational texts of Gothic fiction, and one of the most philosophically enduring works in the English language — the story of a young scientist who creates a living being from assembled body parts and then abandons his creation in horror, setting in motion a tragedy of isolation, longing, and revenge. Born Mary Wollstonecraft Godwin on August 30, 1797, in London, the daughter of the feminist philosopher Mary Wollstonecraft and the political philosopher William Godwin, she was raised in an intellectually rarefied household (though her mother died ten days after her birth) and met the poet Percy Bysshe Shelley when she was fifteen. She eloped with Percy in 1814, travelling to Europe with her stepsister Claire Clairmont; in the summer of 1816, staying at the Villa Diodati on Lake Geneva with Percy, Lord Byron, and John Polidori during the 'Year Without a Summer' caused by the eruption of Mount Tambora, Byron proposed a ghost-story competition; from that competition came Frankenstein — Mary's waking vision of 'a pale student of unhallowed arts kneeling beside the creature he had assembled.' The novel was published anonymously in January 1818 and attributed by many reviewers to Percy Shelley; Mary's authorship was not publicly acknowledged until the 1823 second edition. Percy Shelley died by drowning in 1822; Mary was widowed at twenty-four with a son to raise and spent the next thirty years in England writing, editing Percy's poetry, and supporting herself and her son through literary work. Her other major works include The Last Man (1826), an apocalyptic novel, the novella Mathilda (written 1820, published 1959), and the historical novel Valperga (1823).",
