    bio: "William Shakespeare (1564–1616) is the most influential writer in the English language and the most widely read and performed dramatist in the history of world literature — a poet and playwright who wrote approximately 37 plays, 154 sonnets, and several longer poems during a career in London from roughly 1585 to 1613, whose works have been translated into every major language, performed in every country, and whose influence on English vocabulary, idiom, and literary culture is without parallel. Born in Stratford-upon-Avon on April 23, 1564, to John Shakespeare, a glove-maker and local official, and Mary Arden, the daughter of a prosperous farmer, Shakespeare attended the King's New School in Stratford and married Anne Hathaway in 1582, when he was eighteen and she was twenty-six; they had three children, including twins Hamnet and Judith. By the early 1590s Shakespeare was established in London as an actor and playwright, a member of the Lord Chamberlain's Men (which became the King's Men in 1603 when James I became their patron), and a shareholder in the Globe Theatre, built in 1599. His career falls roughly into periods: early comedies and histories (including The Comedy of Errors, A Midsummer Night's Dream, Much Ado About Nothing, Richard II, Henry V); the great tragedies of the early 1600s (Hamlet, Othello, King Lear, Macbeth — all written between approximately 1600 and 1606); and the late romances (The Winter's Tale, The Tempest). His sonnets, published in 1609 without his authorization, have generated centuries of debate about their biographical meaning. Shakespeare retired to Stratford around 1613 and died on April 23, 1616. The question of his authorship — the 'Shakespeare authorship question' — has been raised periodically since the 19th century; the scholarly consensus is overwhelmingly that William Shakespeare of Stratford wrote the works attributed to him.",
