    name: 'Homer',
    bio: "Homer is the name given to the ancient Greek poet or tradition of poets credited with composing the Iliad and the Odyssey — the two foundational epics of Western literature, composed in oral-formulaic verse probably in the 8th century BCE, though both may draw on traditions stretching back to the Bronze Age events (c. 1200 BCE) they describe. Almost nothing is certain about Homer's biography: ancient sources disagree about where he was born (seven cities claimed him, including Smyrna, Chios, and Colophon), when he lived, whether he was blind (a tradition that probably derives from the self-description of a bard in the Odyssey), and whether the same person composed both epics. The 'Homeric Question' — whether one person or many composed the epics, and how oral composition worked — has been debated by scholars since the 18th century. The Iliad, approximately 15,693 lines of dactylic hexameter, describes approximately 52 days in the tenth year of the Trojan War, centered on the wrath of the Greek hero Achilles after he is dishonored by the commander Agamemnon; it ends with the funeral of the Trojan hero Hector, killed by Achilles in revenge for the death of his companion Patroclus. The Odyssey, approximately 12,109 lines, follows the ten-year journey of Odysseus from Troy back to his home in Ithaca, where his wife Penelope and grown son Telemachus wait; it features the Cyclops, the sorceress Circe, the goddess Calypso, the Sirens, Scylla and Charybdis, and the shades of the dead. Both epics were transmitted orally for centuries before being written down; the Alexandrian scholar Aristarchus of Samothrace established standard texts of both in the 3rd century BCE. The Homeric epics have been translated into every major language; significant English translations include Alexander Pope's verse translations (1715–26), Richmond Lattimore's (1951 and 1965), Robert Fagles's (1990 and 1996), and Emily Wilson's Odyssey (2017) — the first translation of the Odyssey by a woman.",
